<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cottbus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chóśebuz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cottbus (Lower Sorbian: Chóśebuz) is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>. It became part of Brandenburg in 1445 and later developed a strong textile industry. It is the cultural centre of the Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, whose language is still taught there, and is known for Prince Pückler's Branitz Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cottbus.jpg" alt="Cottbus" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Teodor Bordeianu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View-over-cottbus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cottbus, in Lower Sorbian Chóśebuz, lies on the River Spree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>, about 125 km upstream of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. The name comes from the Lower Sorbian personal name Chóśebuz, said to mean cheerful watchman or vigilant hero, so that the place is the settlement of Chóśebud. It was first documented in 1156 as Chotibus, and the spelling Kottbus first appears in 1391.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was established in the tenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> built a castle on a sandy island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>. The March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> captured it in 965, and in 1002 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>. In the German-Polish war of 1015 to 1018 it was an important Polish stronghold, and in 1032 it passed again to the March of Lusatia. In 1514 Jan Rak founded the Universitas Serborum, a Sorbian gymnasium, in the city. In 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, which made Cottbus an enclave of Saxony, and from the 1690s French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloons">Walloons</a> and Palatines settled there.</p><p>After the Second World War Cottbus lay in the Soviet Occupation Zone and then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>, and in 1952 it became capital of the new district of Cottbus. On 17 June 1953 its people joined the uprising against living-standard restrictions and made political demands, and Soviet tanks and workers' militias suppressed it. The city grew with lignite mining and reached its historic peak of 128,943 inhabitants in 1989. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in October 1990 privatisation brought a deep structural change.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cottbus is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, and the political and cultural centre of the Lower Sorbian-speaking Sorbs, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>. Signs are mostly in German and Lower Sorbian, and the Wendish Museum is there. The Brandenburg University of Technology has its seat in the city, and the State Theatre, built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> style by Bernhard Sehring and opened in 1908, is the only state theatre in Brandenburg. Branitz Park, laid out from 1845 by Hermann von Pückler-Muskau, is a landscape park of international significance with two grass pyramids.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cottbus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The land around Lübben, <b>Cottbus</b> and Guben was largely rural, with a strong Sorbian-speaking population.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-lower-lusatia">Margraviate of Lower Lusatia</a></li></ul></section>
